  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Career options for PCB with psychology split into two genuinely different professions, not one blended field: staying on the medical route through NEET and MBBS into psychiatry, or leaving that route for BA/BSc Psychology toward clinical practice, research, or counselling.</strong>
    Both use your Biology background. Only one makes you a doctor who can prescribe medicine. Picking between them without seeing the real cost, timeline, and licensing gap on each side is the single most expensive mistake a PCB student interested in the mind and behaviour can make - and getting it right early is what protects years of your time and moves you toward a stronger skill portfolio and <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>, instead of a slow, expensive detour.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>PCB with psychology really means choosing between two separate professions: psychiatry (NEET-UG, MBBS, then MD/DNB Psychiatry) and psychology (BA/BSc, MA/MSc, then RCI registration only if you want to practise clinically).</li>
      <li>Psychiatry takes roughly 9-12 years to full registration and costs far more, especially through private MBBS; clinical psychology takes roughly 8 years to RCI registration and costs considerably less, but has its own scarce postgraduate seats.</li>
      <li>Your PCB Biology background is a genuine advantage for BSc Psychology, neuropsychology, and behavioural science research - fields that lean on biological and statistical foundations most Arts-entry psychology students do not have.</li>
      <li>NEET is required only for the medical (psychiatry) route. It is not required at any stage to become a psychologist, counsellor, or behavioural researcher.</li>
      <li>The next real step is one small piece of proof - a shadowing stretch, a case profile, or a behavioural-observation project - run through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit years to either side.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

