  <h2 id="why-different">Why PCB with psychology is its own question</h2>

  <p>
    Most career content treats "psychology" as one door and "PCB" as a separate, unrelated door.
  </p>

  <p>
    That misses the actual situation a PCB student interested in the mind and behaviour is in. You already have Biology, which is the real foundation underneath psychiatry, clinical psychology, and neuropsychology alike. The open question is not whether psychology fits a science background - it clearly does - but which of two very different professions you are actually walking toward: one that keeps you inside the NEET-MBBS system, and one that leaves it entirely.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Where the generic advice goes wrong</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Generic "psychology career" content is usually written for Arts-stream entrants and never mentions psychiatry, MBBS, or NEET at all.</li>
      <li>Generic PCB content usually treats psychology as one line item next to nursing and pharmacy, without separating the medical route from the non-medical one.</li>
      <li>Neither version explains that your Biology background is a real, stated eligibility advantage for BSc Psychology and neuropsychology - not just a vague "science helps" claim.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      This page treats PCB with psychology as its own decision, with its own fork, timeline, cost, and licensing rules on each side.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="subject-choice">Taking Psychology alongside PCB in 11th and 12th</h2>

  <p>
    Before the college-level fork, there is a real subject-level question worth clearing up: can you actually study Psychology while doing PCB in school?
  </p>

  <p>
    Under the standard CBSE PCB combination, you take Physics, Chemistry, Biology, and English as your core subjects, plus one more optional subject to complete your five. Many schools also let you add a sixth, purely optional subject on top of that - and Psychology is one of the electives some CBSE schools offer alongside science-stream combinations, especially under the more flexible subject-mixing that NEP 2020 has pushed schools toward.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Not every school offers Psychology as an elective next to PCB - it depends entirely on what your specific school has chosen to run, not on a fixed national rule. Confirm directly with your school's subject list before assuming it is available.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is a practical bonus if it is offered and you score well: CBSE calculates your Class 12 percentage from your best five subjects. If your sixth subject score beats your weakest main-subject score, it automatically replaces that weaker subject in your final percentage - so a strong Psychology score can genuinely protect your overall result, not just satisfy curiosity.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-fork">The real fork: psychiatrist or psychologist</h2>

  <p>
    This is the decision that most PCB-and-psychology content skips entirely, and it is the one that actually matters most.
  </p>

  <p>
    A psychiatrist is a medical doctor who specialised in mental health after completing MBBS. A psychologist studied human behaviour and mental processes through a psychology degree, never went through MBBS, and - even at the highest RCI-registered level - cannot prescribe medication or order medical investigations. These are related fields that often work side by side in the same hospital, but they are legally, financially, and practically two separate professions.
  </p>

